WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:05.588
Toccata in F by
Charles-Marie Widor

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:07.841
[organ music]

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.342
They hear

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.636
an endless variety of color.

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:12.971
[organ music]

00:00:12.971 --> 00:00:14.389
They feel through the floor

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:16.433
the rumble of the deepest bass
pipes.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:17.976
[organ music]

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:19.811
When they hear that,
they'll say,

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:22.772
that is Woolsey Hall
at Yale.

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:25.108
[organ music]

00:00:25.108 --> 00:00:27.026 line:71%
My name is Thomas Murray,

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:31.906 line:76%
and I've been very fortunate
over 38 years to teach graduate

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:35.785
organ performance majors here
on this magnificent organ.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:37.620
The largest pipes in

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.999
the organ are 32 feet
high from the mouth to the top.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:46.087
The four keyboards of this organ
each control a section of it,

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:50.049
and the sections
consist of pipes

00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:52.594
organized by what we call stops.

00:00:53.136 --> 00:00:56.014
There are a lot of people in
the world who don't understand

00:00:56.347 --> 00:00:59.350
the origin of the expression,
pulling out all the stops.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:03.897
We at Yale
have sought to maintain

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.065
this organ in its original form

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.902
without doing things
that would change its style.

00:01:09.194 --> 00:01:12.655
It's what we would call
an orchestral style instrument.

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:15.825
I feel intense delight
in being able

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:18.953
to orchestrate things
to choose the colors that I feel

00:01:19.496 --> 00:01:23.082
are just right for any given
section of any given piece.

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:25.293
Another thing,

00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:29.047
which I feel is the sense
of being in touch with history.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:35.220
This organ was built in stages
and the final stage was 1929.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.265
So, we are hearing the same
sounds that the crowd heard

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.559
when this was dedicated
for the third time.

00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:43.394
[organ music]

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:44.562
The world needs to know

00:01:44.562 --> 00:01:47.524
how special it is, and Yale is
very fortunate to have it.

